  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}=-7\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1} & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4) & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variable\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2,\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$",
